<interrogationes unitas="terminale">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I run a standalone terminal window with this library?
    <responsum symbolum="terminale_argumenta terminale_ambitus pseudoterminale_posix_creare terminale_applicatio_aedificare" via="apps/terminale/terminale.c">Build
      the child yourself: terminale_argumenta (argv; -fumus gives a
      short fixed script, otherwise $SHELL -l, falling back to
      /bin/zsh) and terminale_ambitus (TERM=xterm-256color,
      COLORTERM=truecolor, TERM_PROGRAM=terminale, TMUX and TMUX_PANE
      removed) go into pseudoterminale_posix_creare. Then
      terminale_applicatio_aedificare(app, piscina, intern, pt,
      latitudo, altitudo) builds the store, registries, dispatcher and
      emulator host; app.d and app.figurae feed the window loop.
      apps/terminale/terminale.c is the reference.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I embed a terminal pane inside another app's store, as vicus does?
    <responsum symbolum="terminale_montare terminale_componere" via="lib/vicus_applicatio.c">terminale_montare(app,
      piscina, intern, repo, id, argumenta, systemata, latitudo,
      altitudo) spawns the child itself (argumenta NIHIL = $SHELL -l)
      and mounts the branch with element terminale and the given id
      through the embedded organum manifest. It creates NO
      dispatcher: the host routes events and calls
      terminale_componere with the app as ctx to build the pane's
      components.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>what does my main loop have to call every frame?
    <responsum symbolum="terminale_pulsare aemulator_hospes_pulsare terminale_titulus">The
      library owns no loop. Each frame call terminale_pulsare(app,
      mora_ms): it reads superficies_latitudo / superficies_altitudo
      from the app's branch, resizes the emulator and child when the
      cell grid changed, then pulses the host. Redraw when the result
      has mutatum; finitus means the shell exited. Afterwards
      terminale_titulus(app, &amp;mutatus) gives the OSC 0/2 title,
      with mutatus VERUM once per change.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep the chorda that terminale_titulus returns?
    <responsum symbolum="terminale_titulus">Only until the next pulse.
      It points into a fixed CCLVI-byte buffer inside the app's
      private context (longer titles are cut to CCLVI bytes) and is
      overwritten whenever the program sets a new title. Copy it if
      you need it later; the window app copies it into a stack buffer
      rather than the piscina, because zsh retitles on every prompt
      and a pool copy per change would grow for the whole
      session.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does a key press reach the shell only after the next event arrives?
    <responsum symbolum="codificator_eventa" via="lib/terminale.worklog.md">Real
      input sources send key down, then its text, then key up, but
      the encoder wants the key and its text as one series. So a
      key-down is held as pending; a following committed text event
      is encoded together with it, and any other event (key up,
      mouse, focus, wheel, another key down) first flushes the
      pending key alone. Keys with no text (Enter, Ctrl-C, arrows)
      therefore go out with the next event, normally their own
      key-up.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does Cmd+V or any Cmd key never reach the program?
    <responsum via="lib/terminale.c lib/terminale.worklog.md">Every
      key with MOD_SUPER is dropped, down and up, which is the macOS
      terminal convention (Terminal.app, iTerm). The menu handles the
      shortcut (Cmd+V pastes through it); forwarding the key as well
      made kitty-keyboard programs such as Claude Code type a stray v
      before the pasted text.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why do my program's colours look different from the colours it asked for?
    <responsum via="lib/terminale.c lib/terminale.worklog.md">A
      minimum contrast of 3.0 (WCAG) is applied: text too close to
      its background is nudged the smallest step toward black or
      white that reaches the ratio, because programs assume a dark
      background and the house theme is light. Faint (SGR 2) text is
      first blended halfway into its background, and bold 0-7 uses
      the bright palette. Box drawing and block elements
      (U+2500-259F) skip the contrast floor, since their colour is
      the picture.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is ornamenta_pixelorum and when should it be FALSUM?
    <responsum symbolum="terminale_applicatio_aedificare" via="apps/terminale/terminale_terminalis.c">A
      flag on TerminaleApplicatio, VERUM after
      terminale_applicatio_aedificare. It draws underlines (five
      forms), strike-through, overline, fake bold (glyph drawn again
      one pixel right) and box/block/braille glyphs as pixel shapes.
      The terminal twin (terminale_terminalis) sets it FALSUM because
      its rasteriser turns a horizontal line into box-drawing cells,
      which would erase the letter under an underline.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what happens on failure, and who closes the pseudoterminal?
    <responsum symbolum="terminale_applicatio_aedificare terminale_montare terminale_claudere aemulator_hospes_creare">Once
      the host exists it owns the pseudoterminal, and
      terminale_claudere closes the child through it. A FALSUM from
      terminale_applicatio_aedificare closes pt only when the
      failure comes BEFORE the host is created (bad arguments, no
      memory for the context, or aemulator_hospes_creare failing,
      which closes pt itself). A later failure (organum missing,
      branch refused, store, registries or scratch pool missing)
      returns FALSUM with the child still running, despite the
      header's promise; terminale_montare passes on the same
      result. So zero the TerminaleApplicatio before the call (it is
      zeroed only after the argument check) and call
      terminale_claudere after any FALSUM that left app.hospes
      set.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find which tty the child shell is on?
    <responsum symbolum="terminale_tty">terminale_tty(app) returns the
      child's tty path (for example /dev/ttys011), copied into the
      piscina when the app is built; vicus uses it to match tmux
      clients to panes. It is an empty chorda when the
      pseudoterminal has no tty, such as the in-memory
      bridge.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I mount and close terminals over and over without leaking memory?
    <responsum symbolum="terminale_claudere terminale_montare piscina_generare_dynamicum">Not
      completely. Everything else lives in the piscina you pass, but
      every build also creates its own dynamic scratch piscina
      (terminale_scrutinium, first block LXIV * MXXIV bytes) for
      encoding key bytes, and nothing ever destroys it:
      terminale_claudere only closes the emulator host. vicus mounts
      a new terminale on every pane opening, so each one leaves that
      pool behind for the life of the process.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
